> Bicubic degradation has a phase,negative lobes and antialiasing

A true x2 pair starts from original CIFAR32x32 RGB,downsamples to16x16,
then computes a32x32 bicubic reference from the LR image. HR is never a
network input. Keys a=-.5 uses centers(i+.5)/scale-.5;downsampling widens
the cubic support. Symmetric edges duplicate the boundary sample. Both
passes sum in Float64 and round through Float32 between columns and rows.
Negative lobes and overshoot remain in the raw interpolation.
```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/super_resolution_bicubic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const auto x = n::arange(-2., 2.001, .01, n::DType::Float64), kernel = m::SRCubicTensor(x);
  const auto impulse = n::tensor({0., 0., 1., 0., 0.}, {1, 1, 5, 1}).requires_grad_();
  const auto y = m::SRBicubic(impulse, 1, 10), g = n::grad(n::sum(n::square(y)), {impulse})[0];
  const auto matrix = m::SRContributionMatrix(5, 10);
  std::print("Normalized weight row error {:.3g};input derivative norm {:.6f};raw impulse "
             "minimum {:.6f}.\n",
             n::max(n::abs(n::sum(matrix, {1}) - 1.)).item(), n::sqrt(n::sum(g * g)).item(),
             n::min(y).item());
  auto a = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), b = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  a.lines(x.to_vector<double>(), kernel.to_vector<double>(), "Keys a=-.5", {37, 99, 235})
      .title("Signed cubic kernel")
      .x_label("signed sample offset")
      .y_label("kernel weight")
      .y_limits(-.2, 1.3);
  b.lines(n::arange(0, 10).to_vector<double>(), y.to_vector<double>(), "upsampled impulse",
          {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Half-pixel impulse response")
      .x_label("output sample index")
      .y_label("raw value")
      .y_limits(-.2, 1.2);
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(a).add(b).size(1100, 500).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/super_resolution_bicubic.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Normalized weight row error 0;input derivative norm 3.323882;raw impulse minimum -0.070312.
\image(figures/super_resolution_bicubic.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Signed cubic weights and their half-pixel phase are preserved,including negative lobes. Tensor resampling propagates input derivatives.)
\alttext(Signed cubic weights and their half-pixel phase are preserved,including negative lobes. Tensor resampling propagates input derivatives.)

> CRD subpixel shuffle is an exact permutation

For scale r,the packed input channel is c*r*r+dy*r+dx. A reshape,
transpose and reshape sends it to the corresponding enlarged pixel. No
interpolation or fitted weights occur in this permutation. DCR ordering
would arrange multiple output channels differently. A learned convolution
before the permutation predicts these packed spatial samples.
```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/super_resolution_shuffle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const auto x = n::arange(0, 16).reshape({1, 1, 2, 8}).requires_grad_();
  const auto y = m::SRPixelShuffle(x, 2, 2), up = n::arange(1, 17).reshape(y.shape()),
             g = n::grad(n::sum(y * up), {x})[0];
  std::print("Packed shape1x1x2x8 -> image1x2x4x2;all16 input adjoints finite:{}\n",
             n::all(n::is_finite(g)).item<bool>());
  const auto first = n::select(n::select(y, 0, 0), 2, 0),
             second = n::select(n::select(y, 0, 0), 2, 1);
  auto a = n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(first, {0}),0,15), b = n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(second,{0}),0,15);
  a
      .title("CRD output channel0")
      .x_label("enlarged column")
      .y_label("original row0 at bottom");
  b
      .title("CRD output channel1")
      .x_label("enlarged column")
      .y_label("original row0 at bottom");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(a).add(b).size(1100, 500).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/super_resolution_shuffle.svg");
}
```
